workedExamples.question_text:
The graphs of $f(x)=x+1$ and $g(x)=2-x$ are shown.
(a) Complete the table.
$\begin{array}{c|ccccc}x&-2&-1&0&1&2\\f(x)&&&&&\\g(x)&&&&&\\f(x)+g(x)&&&&&\end{array}$
(b) Sketch their sum and give its equation.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=7mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{curveRed}{HTML}{EF6068}
\draw[black,<->] (-3.3,0)--(3.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,<->] (0,-2.5)--(0,5.5) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \t in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3}{\draw[black] (\t,-0.08)--(\t,0.08);\node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (\t,0) {$\t$};}
\foreach \t in {-2,1,2,3,4,5}{\draw[black] (-0.07,\t)--(0.07,\t);\node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (0,\t) {$\t$};}
\draw[curveBlue,thick,<->] (-3,-2)--(3,4);
\draw[curveRed,thick,<->] (-3,5)--(3,-1);
\node[text=curveBlue,anchor=west] at (1.8,4.2) {$f(x)$};
\node[text=curveRed,anchor=west] at (-3,5.7) {$g(x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
(a) Add the ordinates at each input.
$\begin{array}{c|ccccc}x&-2&-1&0&1&2\\f(x)&-1&0&1&2&3\\g(x)&4&3&2&1&0\\f(x)+g(x)&3&3&3&3&3\end{array}$
(b) Every sum ordinate is $3$, so the sketch is the horizontal line $y=3$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=7mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,<->] (-3.3,0)--(3.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,<->] (0,-1)--(0,4.5) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \t in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3}{\draw[black] (\t,-0.08)--(\t,0.08);\node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (\t,0) {$\t$};}
\draw[black] (-0.07,3)--(0.07,3);
\node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (0,3) {$3$};
\draw[curveBlue,thick,<->] (-3,3)--(3,3);
\foreach \t in {-2,-1,0,1,2}{\fill[curveBlue] (\t,3) circle (1.4pt);}
\node[text=curveBlue,anchor=west] at (0.5,3.6) {$f(x)+g(x)=3$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The component graphs have intercepts $(-2,0)$, $(2,0)$ and $(0,-4)$ for $f$, and $(-\tfrac12,0)$ and $(0,1)$ for $g$. They intersect at $(1-\sqrt6,3-2\sqrt6)$ and $(1+\sqrt6,3+2\sqrt6)$.
Add ordinates at the same input.
$\begin{array}{c|rrrrr}x&-3&-2&-1&0&1\\f(x)&5&0&-3&-4&-3\\g(x)&-5&-3&-1&1&3\\s(x)&0&-3&-4&-3&0\end{array}$
Plot the sum points and join them with an upward-opening parabola. Its minimum is $(-1,-4)$ and its intercepts are $(-3,0)$, $(1,0)$ and $(0,-3)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=4mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{curveRed}{HTML}{EF6068}
\definecolor{curvePurple}{HTML}{8B5CC7}
\draw[black,<->] (-4.2,0)--(4.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,<->] (0,-6)--(0,13) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \t in {-4,-3,-2,-1,1,2,3,4}{\draw[black] (\t,-0.12)--(\t,0.12);\node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (\t,0) {$\t$};}
\foreach \t in {-4,-2,2,4,8,12}{\draw[black] (-0.07,\t)--(0.07,\t);\node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (0,\t) {$\t$};}
\draw[curveBlue,thick,<->,domain=-4:4,samples=100] plot (\x,{(\x)^2-4});
\draw[curveRed,thick,<->] (-3.4,-5.8)--(4,9);
\draw[curvePurple,very thick,<->,domain=-4:3,samples=100] plot (\x,{(\x)^2+2*\x-3});
\foreach \p in {(-3,0),(-2,-3),(-1,-4),(0,-3),(1,0)}{\fill[curvePurple] \p circle (1.4pt);}
\node[below left] at (-1,-4) {$(-1,-4)$};
\node[text=curveBlue] at (-3.2,9) {$f$};
\node[text=curveRed] at (3.5,6) {$g$};
\node[text=curvePurple] at (2.4,10) {$s$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The sum has equation $s(x)=x^2+2x-3$ and domain $\mathbb R$.

The component graphs have intercepts $(-\sqrt2,0)$, $(\sqrt2,0)$ and $(0,-2)$ for $f$, and $(-\tfrac12,0)$ and $(0,1)$ for $g$. They intersect at $(-1,-1)$ and $(3,7)$.
Subtract the ordinate of $g$ from the ordinate of $f$ at each input. Equivalently, reflect $g$ in the $x$-axis and add its ordinates.
$\begin{array}{c|rrrrr}x&-1&0&1&2&3\\f(x)&-1&-2&-1&2&7\\g(x)&-1&1&3&5&7\\d(x)&0&-3&-4&-3&0\end{array}$
Plot the difference points and join them with an upward-opening parabola. Its minimum is $(1,-4)$ and its intercepts are $(-1,0)$, $(3,0)$ and $(0,-3)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=4mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{curveRed}{HTML}{EF6068}
\definecolor{curvePurple}{HTML}{8B5CC7}
\draw[black,<->] (-4.2,0)--(4.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,<->] (0,-6)--(0,11) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \t in {-4,-3,-2,-1,1,2,3,4}{\draw[black] (\t,-0.12)--(\t,0.12);\node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (\t,0) {$\t$};}
\foreach \t in {-4,-2,2,4,6,8,10}{\draw[black] (-0.07,\t)--(0.07,\t);\node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (0,\t) {$\t$};}
\draw[curveBlue,thick,<->,domain=-3.4:3.4,samples=100] plot (\x,{(\x)^2-2});
\draw[curveRed,thick,<->] (-3.4,-5.8)--(4,9);
\draw[curvePurple,very thick,<->,domain=-2.7:4,samples=100] plot (\x,{(\x)^2-2*\x-3});
\foreach \p in {(-1,0),(0,-3),(1,-4),(2,-3),(3,0)}{\fill[curvePurple] \p circle (1.4pt);}
\node[below right] at (1,-4) {$(1,-4)$};
\node[text=curveBlue] at (-3,9) {$f$};
\node[text=curveRed] at (3.7,6.5) {$g$};
\node[text=curvePurple] at (-2.5,6) {$d$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The difference has equation $d(x)=x^2-2x-3$ and domain $\mathbb R$.

The common domain is $x\ne0$, since $9/x$ is undefined at $0$. Add corresponding ordinates separately on each side of $x=0$.
$\begin{array}{c|rrrrrr}x&-9&-3&-1&1&3&9\\x&-9&-3&-1&1&3&9\\9/x&-1&-3&-9&9&3&1\\x+9/x&-10&-6&-10&10&6&10\end{array}$
Join the points smoothly in two separate branches. The positive branch has minimum $(3,6)$; the negative branch has maximum $(-3,-6)$. There are no axis intercepts.
As $x$ approaches $0$, the reciprocal ordinate grows without bound in magnitude, giving the vertical asymptote $x=0$. For large positive or negative $x$, $9/x$ approaches $0$, so the graph approaches the oblique asymptote $y=x$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=3.5mm,y=2.5mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,<->] (-10,0)--(10,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,<->] (0,-12)--(0,12) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[black,dashed] (-9.5,-9.5)--(9.5,9.5);
\node[rotate=36,above] at (7,7) {$y=x$};
\foreach \t in {-9,-3,3,9}{\draw[black] (\t,-0.15)--(\t,0.15);\node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (\t,0) {$\t$};}
\foreach \t in {-10,-6,6,10}{\draw[black] (-0.12,\t)--(0.12,\t);\node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (0,\t) {$\t$};}
\draw[curveBlue,thick,<->,domain=-9.5:-0.85,samples=150] plot (\x,{\x+9/\x});
\draw[curveBlue,thick,<->,domain=0.85:9.5,samples=150] plot (\x,{\x+9/\x});
\fill[curveBlue] (3,6) circle (1.4pt);
\fill[curveBlue] (-3,-6) circle (1.4pt);
\node[below right] at (3,6) {$(3,6)$};
\node[above left] at (-3,-6) {$(-3,-6)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Domain: $\mathbb R\setminus\{0\}$.

Subtract $3$ from every ordinate of $f$.
$\begin{array}{c|rrrrr}x&-3&-1&0&1&3\\f(x)&3&1&0&1&3\\g(x)&3&3&3&3&3\\f(x)-g(x)&0&-2&-3&-2&0\end{array}$
Join the points with straight-line sections. The result is an upward V with vertex $(0,-3)$ and intercepts $(-3,0)$ and $(3,0)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=7mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,<->] (-5,0)--(5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,<->] (0,-4)--(0,2.5) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \t in {-3,-1,1,3}{\draw[black] (\t,-0.07)--(\t,0.07);\node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (\t,0) {$\t$};}
\draw[curveBlue,thick,<->] (-4.5,1.5)--(0,-3)--(4.5,1.5);
\foreach \p in {(-3,0),(0,-3),(3,0)}{\fill[curveBlue] \p circle (1.4pt);}
\node[below right] at (0,-3) {$(0,-3)$};
\node[text=curveBlue,above left] at (-3.5,0.5) {$y=|x|-3$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The equation is $y=|x|-3$, with domain $\mathbb R$ and range $[-3,\infty)$.

Add $3$ to every ordinate of $f$.
$\begin{array}{c|rrrrr}x&-2&-1&0&1&2\\f(x)&4&1&0&1&4\\g(x)&3&3&3&3&3\\f(x)+g(x)&7&4&3&4&7\end{array}$
Plot these points and join them smoothly. The parabola is symmetric about the $y$-axis, has vertex $(0,3)$ and has no $x$-intercepts.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=11mm,y=5mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,<->] (-3,0)--(3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-0.7)--(0,10) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \t in {-2,-1,1,2}{\draw[black] (\t,-0.1)--(\t,0.1);\node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (\t,0) {$\t$};}
\foreach \t in {3,4,7}{\draw[black] (-0.06,\t)--(0.06,\t);\node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (0,\t) {$\t$};}
\draw[curveBlue,thick,<->,domain=-2.5:2.5,samples=100] plot (\x,{(\x)^2+3});
\foreach \p in {(-2,7),(-1,4),(0,3),(1,4),(2,7)}{\fill[curveBlue] \p circle (1.4pt);}
\node[below right] at (0,3) {$(0,3)$};
\node[text=curveBlue,anchor=west] at (0.6,9.5) {$y=x^2+3$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The equation is $y=x^2+3$, with domain $\mathbb R$ and range $[3,\infty)$.

